# Image → LLM context (vision via Codex CLI)

The model is routed through a text-only proxy: images attached or read with the
Read tool **never reach the model**. Never claim to see an image directly.

To turn an image into usable context (describe, summarize, "use as reference",
OCR, analyze a debug screenshot), delegate to the local `codex` CLI (vision
model, auth via the ChatGPT subscription) and only retrieve the **final text** —
the text passes through the proxy.

`codex` runs a modern vision-language model: it reads text, layout, and meaning
in one pass. Treat it as that, not as a bare OCR engine. The quality of the
context you get back is set by the **instruction you send**, so the levers below
matter more than the choice of tool.

## Core principles

The `<INSTRUCTION>` is the only thing you control — make it work for you:

1. **Task-condition every call.** Never send a generic "describe this image".
   Always inject the agent's **current goal/question** into the instruction so
   the model surfaces what *you* need. A goal-aware description beats a generic
   caption by a large margin. Build the instruction as: `<task context> +
   <mode instruction> + <fidelity clause> + <output format>`.
2. **Demand structure.** Ask for sections or JSON (`subject`, `layout`,
   `verbatim_text`, `colors`, `elements[]`) so you can parse the result reliably
   instead of re-reading prose.
3. **Forbid guessing (critical for any verbatim/OCR/code/IDs).** You have no
   pixels to verify against — the model's text is trusted blindly. Vision models
   hallucinate plausible text (code, hashes, version numbers, identifiers) from
   language priors. Always append: *"Transcribe text exactly as visible. If a
   character is blurred, occluded, or unreadable, mark it `[?]` — never guess and
   never autocorrect to plausible text. State your uncertainty explicitly."*
4. **Separate observation from inference.** Ask the model to label what it
   **sees** vs what it **infers**, so you can weight each accordingly.
5. **Allow a second round-trip.** Default to one task-conditioned call. If the
   returned context misses a detail you need, re-invoke with a sharper, narrower
   question rather than forcing one giant prompt. Reserve a full question-loop
   for dense charts/dashboards/UIs.
6. **Stay reproducible.** The returned text becomes a lossy artifact the model
   trusts. For the same image + same question, reuse the previous output instead
   of re-running (the call is non-deterministic); re-run only when the question
   changes.

## Pre-check

Verify that `codex` is available first:

```bash
command -v codex
```

Missing or logged out (`codex login`) → **STOP** and say so. Never substitute
another vision API and never invent the image content.

## Command

Windows → **Git Bash**, not WSL (WSL bash cannot launch the Windows `codex`, no
`node`). Write the output **outside the workspace** (system temp), so it never
pollutes any repo the rule is ported into:

```bash
& "C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe" -lc 'printf "%s" "<INSTRUCTION>" | codex exec --skip-git-repo-check --sandbox read-only --config mcp_servers="{}" -o "$TMP/codex-ctx.txt" -i "/c/absolute/path/image.png" -'
```

From a bash shell directly:

```bash
printf '%s' '<INSTRUCTION>' | codex exec --skip-git-repo-check --sandbox read-only --config mcp_servers='{}' -o "$TMP/codex-ctx.txt" -i "/c/abs/image.png" -
```

Then read the file with the Read tool (Windows path, e.g.
`C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\Temp\codex-ctx.txt`) to retrieve the context.

## Rules

- Multiple images: repeat `-i "/c/abs/img.png"` for each one, in a single call.
- `-o <file>` isolates the final message (clean, proxy-safe output). Write it
  outside the workspace (`$TMP/codex-ctx.txt`) then read it back with Read.
  Otherwise, read the last message printed on stdout.
- `--config mcp_servers="{}"` disables `codex` MCP servers and avoids the noisy
  `ERROR rmcp::transport::worker ... connection timeout` lines on stderr. If
  they show up anyway, ignore them: no impact on the result.
- Prompt passed on **stdin** with a trailing `-` (avoids quoting pitfalls).
  Wrap `<INSTRUCTION>` in single quotes; do not pass it as a positional argument.
- Image paths must be **absolute**, in Git Bash style (`/c/Users/...`).
- Never use `OPENAI_API_KEY`; this path goes through the ChatGPT subscription.
- Expect ~30–60 s per call (vision model, several thousand tokens).

## Mode instructions (`<INSTRUCTION>`)

These are **modes**, not standalone prompts. Pick one as the base, then prepend
the **current task/goal** and append the **fidelity clause** + an **output
format** (see Core principles). `Generic` is the fallback when no mode fits.

| Mode | Base instruction |
| --- | --- |
| Generic | `Describe this image factually and structured so another LLM can use it as context: subject, layout, colors, verbatim text, style. Be concise.` |
| Character / art ref | `Describe this character reference: silhouette, hair, outfit (pieces/colors/materials), accessories/weapon, palette, pose, render style.` |
| UI / mockup | `Describe this UI: regions, components, verbatim labels, color/spacing cues, interaction hints. Separate what you SEE from what you INFER.` |
| OCR / verbatim | `Extract ALL text verbatim, preserving reading order. Output only the text. Transcribe exactly as visible; mark any unreadable character [?]; never guess, never autocorrect.` |
| Debug screenshot | `Describe this editor/app screenshot: screen, panels, verbatim error/log text, selected elements, current state. Quote error/log text exactly; mark unreadable parts [?].` |

### Composition example

For "find why this dialog looks broken" on a UI screenshot, the assembled
`<INSTRUCTION>` is:

```text
Task: I'm debugging why a settings dialog renders with overlapping buttons.
Describe this UI: regions, components, verbatim labels, color/spacing cues,
interaction hints. Separate what you SEE from what you INFER.
Transcribe any text exactly as visible; mark unreadable characters [?]; never
guess. Return JSON: { subject, layout, elements:[{label, role, position,
issue?}], verbatim_text, observed_vs_inferred }.
```
